Design a 4-to-16 line decoder that takes two 2-bit inputs, A and B, and produces a one-hot output on a 16-bit vector O. For each combination of the input bits, the corresponding bit in the output is set to HIGH while all other bits remain LOW. The code includes a default case to ensure O outputs a LOW signal if the input exceeds defined cases. This implementation allows for simplified addressing in digital circuits, enabling selection among 16 different outputs based on the input combinations.
